## 1 Introduction

Gravitational-wave (GW) astronomy has opened up a new window to the Universe and led to unprecedented insights (Abbott et al., 2016, 2016, 2017, 2018). With almost 100 GW detections of binary neutron-star (BNS), binary black-hole (BBH) and neutron star-black hole (NS-BH) mergers (Abbott et al., 2019, 2021; The LIGO Scientific Collaboration et al., 2021), the mass distribution of stellar-mass BHs is being revealed across cosmic time. This will help to better understand many aspects relevant to the formation of BHs such as supernova (SN) explosion physics, and pre-SN evolution of massive single and binary stars. Previously, only \\mathcal{O}(10) stellar-mass BHs were known, mostly residing in Galactic X-ray binaries (Casares et al., 2017) with Cygnus X-1 hosting the most massive BH of \\approx21\\,\\mathrm{M}_{\\odot}(Miller-Jones et al., 2021).

The chirp mass of GW merger events,

\\mathcal{M}=\\frac{(m_{1}m_{2})^{3/5}}{(m_{1}+m_{2})^{1/5}},  with component masses m_{1} and m_{2}, is one of the most accurately known GW observables (Tiwari and Fairhurst, 2021). Since the observing run O3a of the Advanced LIGO and Advanced Virgo instruments, hints of a gap in the chirp-mass distribution at 10-12\\,\\mathrm{M}_{\\odot} and peaks at 8, 14, 27 and 45\\,\\mathrm{M}_{\\odot} have been reported (Tiwari and Fairhurst, 2021). They are corroborated by measurements in O3b (The LIGO Scientific Collaboration et al., 2021; Tiwari, 2022) and have complementary features in the inferred BH mass distribution at about 9, 16, 30 and 57\\,\\mathrm{M}_{\\odot}(Tiwari and Fairhurst, 2021; The LIGO Scientific Collaboration et al., 2021; Tiwari, 2022; Edelman et al., 2023). Given the current number of BBH merger detections, the peaks at 9 and 30\\,\\mathrm{M}_{\\odot} seem robustwhereas the significance of features around \\approx 16\\,\\mathrm{M}_{\\odot} is less clear (Talbot and Thrane, 2018; Sadiq et al., 2022; Wong and Cranmer, 2022; Edelman et al., 2023; Farah et al., 2023).

BBH mergers are theoretically expected to form through isolated binary evolution and dynamically in dense stellar environments (Mapelli, 2021; Kocsis, 2022; Mandel and Broekgaarden, 2022). In the former channel1 (Fig. 1), the first- and second-born BH both originate from stars that have been stripped of their envelopes by stable binary mass transfer or a common-envelope phase (called binary-stripped stars, BSSs, from hereon). Dynamically-formed BBH mergers additionally invoke BHs from single stars and repeated BH mergers.

Footnote 1: We do not consider chemically-homogenous evolution because it leads to BBH mergers with chirp masses \\gtrsim 20 M{}_{\\odot} that are beyond the masses of interest in this work (Marchant et al., 2016; Mandel and de Mink, 2016; du Buisson et al., 2020).

Envelope removal in BSSs is known to affect the core structures of stars such that they form NSs and BHs of different masses compared to single stars (Timmes et al., 1996; Wellstein and Langer, 1999; Brown et al., 2001; Podsiadlowski et al., 2004; Woosley, 2019; Ertl et al., 2020; Schneider et al., 2021; Laplace et al., 2021). In this paper, we compute the birth distributions of BH masses from BSSs and single stars at different metallicities. We show that the peaks around the chirp-mass gap and the corresponding peaks in the BH-mass distribution are a natural outcome of envelope stripping in binary stars.

## 2 Methods

We use published models of single and BSSs at solar metallicity (Schneider et al., 2021), Z=\\mathrm{Z}_{\\odot}=0.0142, and newly compute models at \\mathrm{Z}_{\\odot}/10 following the methodology of Schneider et al. (2021). We employ the stellar evolution code MESA (revision 10398, Paxton et al., 2011, 2013, 2015, 2018, 2019) and evolve models from the beginning of core-hydrogen burning until an iron core forms that collapses at velocities of > 950 \\mathrm{km\\,s^{-1}}. The pre-SN stellar structures are then used as input to the neutrino-driven, semi-analytic SN code of Muller et al. (2016) to determine whether stars explode in SNe or collapse to BHs. We apply calibrations as in Schneider et al. (2021), assume that the entire stellar mass falls into the BH, and use a maximum NS mass of 2 M{}_{\\odot}. The initial masses of stars are chosen to avoid (pulsational) pair-instability SNe (for further details and all stellar model data see Appendix A).

The pre-SN core structures of BSSs depend on the timing of mass transfer (Schneider et al., 2021). We consider the removal of hydrogen-rich stellar envelopes during core hydrogen burning (Case A), between the end of core hydrogen and core helium burning (Case B), and after core helium exhaustion (Case C). Case B is further divided into early and late for donors with radiative and convective envelopes, respectively. Because Case A/B BSSs, and single stars/Case-C BSSs result in similar pre-SN core structures (Schneider et al., 2021), we simplify our figures and only show results for single stars and Case-B BSSs.

## 3 Pre-Supernova Stellar Structures

The delayed, neutrino-driven SN mechanism is the leading idea on how the collapse of the core of a massive star is reversed and leads to a supernova explosion (e.g. May and White, 1966; Colgate and White, 1966; Arnett, 1967; Fryer and Warren, 2002; Mezzacappa, 2005; Janka et al., 2007; Iwakami et al., 2008; Takiwaki et al., 2014; Muller, 2016; Janka et al., 2016; O'Connor and Couch, 2018; O'Connor et al., 2018; Burrows et al., 2020; Burrows and Vartanyan, 2021). For the explodability of stars within this framework, an often-used proxy is the compactness of their cores at core collapse2,

Footnote 2: For example, in magneto-rotational and other SN explosion mechanisms, the compactness parameter may not be a useful proxy for explodability.

\\xi_{M}=\\frac{M/{\\rm M_{\\odot}}}{R(M)/1000\\,{\\rm km}},  where R(M) is the radius at mass coordinate M, usually taken as M=2.5\\,{\\rm M_{\\odot}}(O'Connor and Ott, 2011). Low compactness \\xi_{2.5} suggests that stars explode while high values indicate unsuccessful explosions and collapse to BHs (O'Connor and Ott, 2011; Ugliano et al., 2012; Sukhbold and Woosley, 2014; Ertl et al., 2016; Sukhbold et al., 2016; Muller et al., 2016; Chieffi and Limongi, 2020; Patton and Sukhbold, 2020; Schneider et al., 2021).

The compactness of massive stars and hence their final fates are mainly set by the carbon-oxygen (CO) core mass M_{\\rm CO} and the central carbon mass fraction X_{\\rm C}_at the end of core helium burning_(Chieffi and Limongi, 2020; Patton and Sukhbold, 2020; Schneider et al., 2021). Stellar evolution beyond helium burning is driven by thermal neutrino losses that depend mostly on the density and temperature in the stellar cores, which are set by M_{\\rm CO}. Nuclear energy generation of the subsequent carbon burning additionally depends on the amount of available fuel, i.e. X_{\\rm C}.

In helium burning, three \\alpha-particles fuse into carbon and, once carbon is available, \\alpha-capture via the {}^{12}{\\rm C}(\\alpha,\\gamma)^{16}{\\rm O} nuclear reaction produces oxygen and consumes carbon. The number of available \\alpha-particles further depends on the growth of the convective core during helium burning which is closely linked to shell hydrogen burning. In stars with more massive cores, X_{\\rm C} is smaller such that carbon burning eventually becomes neutrino-dominated and turns radiative at a certain M_{\\rm CO}. Enhanced core contraction then leads to high compactness (Sukhbold and Woosley, 2014; Chieffi and Limongi, 2020; Patton and Sukhbold, 2020; Schneider et al., 2021). At somewhat higher M_{\\rm CO}, neon and oxygen burning occur earlier, decreasing the compactness such that a compactness peak forms (see below; Laplace et al., 2023). For even higher M_{\\rm CO}, also neon burning becomes neutrino-dominated, again sharply increasing the compactness (Sukhbold and Woosley, 2014; Chieffi and Limongi, 2020; Patton and Sukhbold, 2020; Schneider et al., 2021).

At {\\rm Z_{\\odot}}, this leads to compactness peaks in single stars and BSSs at M_{\\rm CO}\\approx 7\\,{\\rm M_{\\odot}} and 8\\,{\\rm M_{\\odot}}, respectively, where carbon burning becomes neutrino-dominated, and an increase of \\xi_{2.5} at M_{\\rm CO}\\approx 13\\,{\\rm M_{\\odot}} and 15\\,{\\rm M_{\\odot}}, respectively, where also neon burning becomes neutrino-dominated (Fig. 2a; see also Schneider et al., 2021). The M_{\\rm CO}-shifts are caused by a larger X_{\\rm C} in BSSs compared to single stars, because the convective helium-burning cores of BSSs grow less in mass as a consequence of having lost the hydrogen-rich envelopes by binary mass transfer (Brown et al., 1996, 2001; Podsiadlowski et al., 2004; Woosley, 2019; Chieffi and Limongi, 2020; Sukhbold and Adams, 2020; Patton and Sukhbold, 2020; Schneider et al., 2021; Laplace et al., 2021, 2023). At {\\rm Z_{\\odot}}/10, we find the same qualitative \\xi_{2.5}-M_{\\rm CO} relations (Fig. 2b).

Despite the difference of a factor of 10 in metallicity, we find that the CO-core masses at the compactness peaks are similar (M_{\\rm CO}\\approx 7.5\\,{\\rm M_{\\odot}}); the peak of single stars is shifted by 0.5\\,{\\rm M_{\\odot}} to lower M_{\\rm CO} compared to the {\\rm Z_{\\odot}} models and by 0.8\\,{\\rm M_{\\odot}} in BSSs (Fig. 2). At lower metallicity with weaker winds, the convective helium-burning core grows relatively more in mass than at higher metallicity such that more \\alpha particles are mixed into it and more carbon is burnt into oxygen.

With lower X_{\\rm C}, the compactness peaks shift to smaller M_{\\rm CO} (Fig. 2).

At Z={\\rm Z_{\\odot}}/10, the second major increase in compactness of single stars is at almost the same M_{\\rm CO} of \\approx 13\\,{\\rm M_{\\odot}} as in the {\\rm Z_{\\odot}} models. In BSSs at {\\rm Z_{\\odot}}/10, the transition is also at M_{\\rm CO}\\approx 13\\,{\\rm M_{\\odot}}, but is smaller by 2\\,{\\rm M_{\\odot}} at {\\rm Z_{\\odot}}. At the higher metallicity, wind mass loss during core helium burning in BSSs with M_{\\rm CO}\\gtrsim 10\\,{\\rm M_{\\odot}} is so strong that the entire helium layer and parts of the CO-rich layer are lost, delaying the compactness-increase to higher M_{\\rm CO} and reducing the total stellar mass.

## 4 Compact-Remnant Masses

Applying our SN model, we find that stars in the compactness peak at M_{\\rm CO} \\approx 7-8 {\\rm M_{\\odot}} and those with high compactness at M_{\\rm CO} > 13-15 {\\rm M_{\\odot}} produce BHs (Fig. 3). The same conclusion is reached by applying other explodability criteria based on neutrino-driven SNe: a compactness threshold of 0.44 predicts the same explodability as in our analysis in >95\\% of cases and the two-parameter explodability criterion of Ertl et al. (2016) agrees in >92\\% of cases (Appendix A). NS masses reach up to 2\\,{\\rm M_{\\odot}}, where the lowest masses are from stars with the smallest compactness. In single stars, NS and BH formation occur at almost the same initial masses for both metallicities (at M_{\\rm ini} \\lesssim 35 {\\rm M_{\\odot}} and outside the compactness peak at M_{\\rm ini} \\approx 22 {\\rm M_{\\odot}}; Fig. 3). In BSSs, we find NS formation at {\\rm Z_{\\odot}} for initial masses of up to about 70 {\\rm M_{\\odot}} while this is up to about 40 {\\rm M_{\\odot}} at {\\rm Z_{\\odot}}/10.

In general, at higher Z, winds are stronger such that the BH masses are smaller. Because BSSs additionally lose their hydrogen-rich envelopes, their final and thus BH masses are smaller than those of single stars (Fig. 3).

The BH masses of BSSs in the compactness peaks at {\\rm Z_{\\odot}} and {\\rm Z_{\\odot}}/10 are 8.7-9.8 {\\rm M_{\\odot}}, with the BHs at {\\rm Z_{\\odot}}/10 being more massive by about 0.6 {\\rm M_{\\odot}} (Fig. 3). As shown above, the compactness peaks occur at a certain M_{\\rm CO}, which is set by the size of the convective helium-burning core. This in turn is given by the total mass of the BSS. Because the CO-core masses M_{\\rm CO} at the end of core helium of BSSs in the compactness peak at {\\rm Z_{\\odot}} and {\\rm Z_{\\odot}}/10 are similar, this implies that also the total helium-star mass and thus the BH mass must be similar, regardless of the exact mass loss history. Compactness-peak BHs from BSSs thus have a characteristic mass across metallicity. The above argument no longer holds if mass loss during core helium burning reduces the helium core mass. The latter can happen in stars with Z>{\\rm Z_{\\odot}}, and, beyond some Z, BH formation may no longer be possible (Heger et al., 2003).

The same argument holds for the BH masses of BSSs of \\approx 16 {\\rm M_{\\odot}} at the second major compactness-increase (Fig. 3). We have thus identified two characteristic BH masses from BSSs of \\approx 9 {\\rm M_{\\odot}} and \\approx 16 {\\rm M_{\\odot}} that we call "low-mass" (BH{}_{\\rm L}) and "high-mass" (BH{}_{\\rm H}) BHs from hereon.

In about 10-20% of our {\\rm Z_{\\odot}}/10 models beyond the compactness peak, the SN is not energetic enough to unbind the entire stellar envelope and SN fallback is expected. At {\\rm Z_{\\odot}}, this fraction is 20-40%. It remains unknown how much mass falls back and hence what is the resulting BH mass. Furthermore, some BH-forming stars may experience mass loss at core collapse because of neutrino losses leading to a weak SN-like transient (Nadezhin, 1980; Lovegrove & Woosley, 2013), and some BHs may accrete mass from their companion star during later binary-star evolution (c.f. Fig. 1). Such mechanisms are not accounted for in our models and can affect the BH masses.

## 5 Black-hole mass distribution

Assuming a Salpeter initial-mass function (Salpeter, 1955) for single stars and primary stars in binaries, and that initial binary mass ratios and logarithmic orbital separations follow uniform distributions (Opik, 1924), we compute the mass spectrum of BHs (Fig. 4). We mark single stars that spent more than 5\\times 10^{4} yr in regions of the Hertzsprung-Russell diagram where S-Doradus luminous blue variables (LBVs) are found (Smith et al., 2004; Davies et al., 2018) because they likely experience unaccounted for mass loss. They may lose their entire envelope and behave like BSSs.

Not considering SN fallback, the BH mass distributions at \\rm Z_{\\odot} and \\rm Z_{\\odot}/10 are bimodal with a pronounced BH-mass gap and the lowest-mass BHs are from stars in the compactness peak (Fig. 4). In single stars, BHs are more massive than \\approx 12 \\rm M_{\\odot} and the gap is at \\approx 14-17 \\rm M_{\\odot} for \\rm Z_{\\odot} and at \\approx 20-27 \\rm M_{\\odot} for \\rm Z_{\\odot}/10. The masses of the compactness-peak BHs (\\approx 9 \\rm M_{\\odot}) and the position of the BH mass gap at \\approx 10-15 \\rm M_{\\odot} are similar in BSSs at both metallicities. Compactness-peak BHs (BH{}_{\\rm L}) and BHs from the second major compactness increase (BH{}_{\\rm H}) can thus explain the inferred peaks at 9 and 16 \\rm M_{\\odot} in the BH mass distribution of BBH mergers (Tiwari and Fairhurst, 2021; The LIGO Scientific Collaboration et al., 2021; Tiwari, 2022).

The BH mass spectrum of BSSs at \\gtrsim20 \\rm M_{\\odot} depends on metallicity because of Z-dependent WR winds (Fig. 4; Vink and de Koter, 2005; Belczynski et al., 2010; Spera et al., 2015). At \\rm Z_{\\odot}, the maximum BH mass in our models barely reaches 20 \\rm M_{\\odot} while it is almost 45 \\rm M_{\\odot} at \\rm Z_{\\odot}/10. This limits the maximum mass of BHs in X-ray binaries formed via binary-star evolution, and naturally explains why no BH with higher mass than in Cygnus X-1 has been found in the Milky Way (21.2 \\pm 2.2 \\rm M_{\\odot}, Miller-Jones et al., 2021).

Assuming that 50% of a star's envelope falls back, BSS BHs in the range 5-8 \\rm M_{\\odot} and 6-9 \\rm M_{\\odot} form in our \\rm Z_{\\odot} and \\rm Z_{\\odot}/10 models, respectively (Fig. 4). Fallback is thus required to explain the BH masses near the observationally-suggested NS-BH mass gap at 2-5 \\rm M_{\\odot}(Ozel et al., 2010; Farr et al., 2011; Wyrzykowski and Mandel, 2020; Schneider et al., 2021).

## 6 Chirp-mass distribution

The two newly-identified, characteristic BH masses of BSSs of \\approx 9 \\rm M_{\\odot} (BH{}_{\\rm L}) and \\gtrsim 16 \\rm M_{\\odot} (BH{}_{\\rm H}) may lead to three features in the chirp-mass distribution of BBH mergers (BH{}_{\\rm L}+BH{}_{\\rm L}, BH{}_{\\rm H}+BH{}_{\\rm H}, and BH{}_{\\rm H}+BH{}_{\\rm H}). At \\rm Z_{\\odot}, they are at \\mathcal{M}\\approx 7.5-8.1 \\rm M_{\\odot}, 9.9-11.7 \\rm M_{\\odot} and 13.2-17.3 \\rm M_{\\odot}, while they are at \\mathcal{M}\\approx 7.9-8.9 \\rm M_{\\odot}, 10.7-18.1 \\rm M_{\\odot} and 14.6-42.2 \\rm M_{\\odot} at \\rm Z_{\\odot}/10 (Fig. 5). SN-fallback BHs will broaden the first contribution and extend it to the observed NS-BH mass gap.

Isolated binary-star evolution suppresses BH{}_{\\rm L}+BH{}_{\\rm H} mergers (Schneider et al., 2021, Appendix B). When forming the less-massive BH{}_{\\rm L} from stars in the compactness peak first, the initially less massive companion star cannot gain enough mass by mass transfer to result in a high-mass BH{}_{\\rm H}. A high-mass BH{}_{\\rm H} must form first, but then the parameter space for the companion to fall into the compactness peak and give rise to a low-mass BH{}_{\\rm L} is significantly smaller than forming another high-mass BH{}_{\\rm H}. Therefore, our models of BSSs predict a dearth of BBH mergers at chirp masses of about 9-13\\,\\rm M_{\\odot}.

## 7 Neutron star-black hole and double neutron-star mergers

We predict NS+BH{}_{\\rm L} mergers with \\mathcal{M}\\approx 2.7-3.7\\,\\rm M_{\\odot} and NS+BH{}_{\\rm H} mergers with \\mathcal{M}\\approx 3.6-7.0\\,\\rm M_{\\odot} from our BSS models for a minimum NS mass of 1.3\\,\\rm M_{\\odot} and a maximum BH mass of 45\\,\\rm M_{\\odot}. As explained above, forming the BHs in NS-BH mergers first is favored by binary-star evolution. Hence, NS-BH mergers with BH masses of 9\\,\\rm M_{\\odot} are predicted to occur more frequently than those with >16\\,\\rm M_{\\odot} BHs because of the stellar initial mass function.

The NS-BH mergers GW200105 with component masses 8.9^{+1.2}_{-1.5}\\,\\rm M_{\\odot} and 1.9^{+0.3}_{-0.2}\\,\\rm M_{\\odot}, and GW200115 with 5.7^{+1.8}_{-2.1}\\,\\rm M_{\\odot} and 1.5^{+0.7}_{-0.3}\\,\\rm M_{\\odot}(Abbott et al., 2021b) appear consistent with a compactness-peak and a SN-fallback BH, respectively. The high mass ratio event GW190814 of a 22.2-24.3\\,\\rm M_{\\odot} BH and a 2.50-2.67\\,\\rm M_{\\odot} compact object (Abbott et al., 2020b) might be an example of a high-mass BH{}_{\\rm H} merging with a very massive NS. Double NS mergers are possible with a total mass of up to 4\\,\\rm M_{\\odot} in our models, consistent with GW190425 (total mass of 3.4^{+0.3}_{-0.1}\\,\\rm M_{\\odot}, Abbott et al. 2020a).

## 8 Discussion and conclusion

Predicting merger rates requires population-synthesis modelling including the cosmological star-formation history and thus the contributions of stellar populations of different Z. This is beyond the scope of this work. Nevertheless, because the BH{}_{\\rm L} and BH{}_{\\rm H} masses of BSSs are so similar across a large range of metallicity, we predict peaks in the observed BH and chirp-mass distribution. This is in agreement with the location of the observed chirp-mass gap at 10-12\\,\\rm M_{\\odot} and also the peaks at 8 and 14\\,\\rm M_{\\odot} in the chirp-mass distribution of 70 BBH mergers to date3 (Fig. 5).

Footnote 3: Data taken from catalogues “GWTC-1-confident”, “GWTC-2.1-confident” and “GWTC-3-confident”, accessed via [https://www.gw-openscience.org](https://www.gw-openscience.org) (Abbott et al., 2021c).

The peaks and gap in the BH mass distribution of single stars vary strongly with Z and can thus not give rise to the peaks and gap in the observed chirp-mass distribution. Moreover, BBH mergers from single stars populate the observed chirp-mass gap and rather contribute at chirp masses \\gtrsim13\\,\\rm M_{\\odot} (Fig. 5). Hence, the low chirp-mass regime in our models is dominated by BHs from BSSs regardless of whether the BBH mergers are from the isolated-binary or dynamical-formation channel. This is not so surprising as BSSs naturally have lower masses than single stars and thus also form less massive BHs. Furthermore, this interpretation is in line with models of dynamically-formed BBH mergers in dense stellar environments that have their dominant contribution at higher masses and underpredict the observed rate at \\approx 10 M{}_{\\odot}(Rodriguez et al., 2019; Yang et al., 2019; Antonini et al., 2023). Hierarchical (repeated) BH mergers of BH{}_{\\rm L} and BH{}_{\\rm H} may help explain the observed peaks at 30 and 57 M{}_{\\odot} in the BH mass distribution (Tiwari and Fairhurst, 2021; Tiwari, 2022; Mahapatra et al., 2022), and other mechanisms (e.g. binary evolution) may add further features (van Son et al., 2022) but leave our predicted characteristic BH masses unaltered.

The compactness landscape found in this work with a peak at M_{\\rm CO} \\approx 7-8 M{}_{\\odot} and high compactness at M_{\\rm CO} \\gtrsim 13 M{}_{\\odot} agrees with the results obtained by other groups using different stellar evolution codes and/or making different physics assumptions (see e.g. Sukhbold and Woosley, 2014; Chieffi and Limongi, 2020; Patton and Sukhbold, 2020; Schneider et al., 2021; Takahashi et al., 2023). Moreover, Woosley (2019) and Aguilera-Dena et al. (2023) model helium stars at different metallicities as proxies of BSSs and their results confirm that high compactness is found at characteristic M_{\\rm CO} for a large range of metallicities and that the final (i.e. BH) masses of the helium stars at these M_{\\rm CO} are also similar, as in our work. Ertl et al. (2020) study the collapse of the solar-metallicity helium stars of Woosley (2019) with their one-dimensional, neutrino-driven supernova code and confirm our results of a bimodal BH mass distribution with a large \\approx 9 M{}_{\\odot} BH component from compactness-peak BSSs and a gap at about 11 M{}_{\\odot}(see also Woosley et al., 2020).

The characteristic BH masses of BSSs of 9 and 16 M{}_{\\odot} and hence their bimodal BH mass spectrum depend on stellar, SN, and nuclear physics (see also Appendix C). Besides metallicity and wind strength (see above), the masses also depend, e.g., on convective core-boundary mixing and the {}^{12}C(\\alpha,\\gamma)^{16}O nuclear reaction rate. While convective boundary mixing during core hydrogen burning mainly affects the number of BHs as it relates helium core masses to initial masses, the size of the convective helium-burning core directly affects the relation of CO-core to BH mass. Changing the convective core overshooting in our single-star models by a factor of 2 results in a 0.3-0.5 M{}_{\\odot} shift in M_{\\rm CO} of the compactness peak and thus a change in the BH masses of BSSs by a similar amount. The {}^{12}C(\\alpha,\\gamma)^{16}O reaction controls X_{\\rm C} and thus at which CO-core masses stars form BHs. Using the slower rate of Kunz et al. (2002) leads to an increase of the compactness-peak M_{\\rm CO} by \\approx 0.9 M{}_{\\odot}. This reaction is also important for the pair-instability-SN BH mass gap (Farmer et al., 2020). The chirp-mass distribution will help constrain it and other essential physics determining the formation of stellar-mass BHs.

From GW detections of compact-object mergers, one can directly infer their luminosity distances and use them as "standard sirens" (Schutz, 1986; Chernoff and Finn, 1993; Holz and Hughes, 2005). Using universal features in the compact-object mass distribution, such as predicted here, one can then obtain the redshift to the sources and hence measure the cosmological redshift-luminosity distance relation. This allows for an independent determination of the Hubble constant (Farr et al., 2019; Ezquiaga and Holz, 2022). The characteristic BH masses identified here may thus help better understand the expansion history of the Universe.

