Next, we consider that the more massive BH{}_{\\rm H} forms first. This requires initial masses of M_{\\rm ini,1}>70\\,{\\rm M}_{\\odot} (M_{\\rm ini,1}>40\\,{\\rm M}_{\\odot}) because star 1 will otherwise produce a NS or a low-mass BH{}_{\\rm L} (Fig. 3). As before, we assume that the initial masses of the secondary star, M_{\\rm ini,2}, are drawn from a uniform mass ratio distribution, i.e. all M_{\\rm ini,2} are equally likely for a given M_{\\rm ini,1}. For an arbitrary, fixed mass-transfer efficiency, there is only a very limited range of initial masses for star 2, \\Delta M, (M_{\\rm ini,2}<M_{\\rm ini,1}) to obtain a mass of M_{2}\\approx 35\\,{\\rm M}_{\\odot}\\pm\\Delta M (M_{2}\\approx 25\\,{\\rm M}_{\\odot}\\pm\\Delta M) after the first mass-transfer phase such that star 2 falls into the compactness peak and then leaves behind a low-mass BSS BH{}_{\\rm L}. In the majority of cases, star 2 has a different mass and does not fall into the compactness peak such that it forms a BSS NS or BH{}_{\\rm H} after the second mass-transfer phase. Let M_{\\rm min} and M_{\\rm max} be the minimum and maximum (initial) mass of a star to form a NS or BH, respectively, and M_{\\rm BH_{H}} the (initial) mass threshold for BH{}_{\\rm H} formation. The probability of forming a compactness-peak BH{}_{\\rm L} after the second mass transfer is then \\Delta M/(M_{\\rm max}-M_{\\rm min}) while the probabilities to form a NS and BH{}_{\\rm H} are (M_{\\rm BH_{H}}-M_{\\rm min}-\\Delta M)/(M_{\\rm max}-M_{\\rm min}) and (M_{\\rm max}-M_{\\rm BH_{H}})/(M_{\\rm max}-M_{\\rm min}), respectively. BH{}_{\\rm L}+BH{}_{\\rm H} mergers where BH{}_{\\rm H} forms first are thus suppressed compared to NS+BH{}_{\\rm H} and BH{}_{\\rm H}+BH{}_{\\rm H} mergers by factors of (M_{\\rm BH_{H}}-M_{\\rm min}-\\Delta M)/\\Delta M and (M_{\\rm max}-M_{\\rm BH_{H}})/\\Delta M, respectively. Assuming M_{\\rm min}\\approx 10\\,{\\rm M}_{\\odot}, M_{\\rm max}\\approx 100\\,{\\rm M}_{\\odot}, \\Delta M\\approx 5\\,{\\rm M}_{\\odot} and taking M_{\\rm BH_{H}}\\approx 70\\,{\\rm M}_{\\odot} at Z{}_{\\odot} and \\approx 40\\,{\\rm M}_{\\odot} at Z{}_{\\odot}/10, we find suppression factors of about 5-10. We conclude that BH{}_{\\rm L}+BH{}_{\\rm H} mergers are not forbidden but suppressed in our models, and the models predict that some BBHs in the 10-12\\,{\\rm M}_{\\odot} chirp-mass gap should be detected in the future.

## Appendix C Influence of various physics assumptions on the BH mass spectrum

The exact bimodal structure of the BH mass spectrum of single and BSSs depends on various physical processes and as such offers the possibility to better understand and constrain them with the help of observations. In Fig. 6, we schematically show how the masses of the low-mass BH{}_{\\rm L} and high-mass BH{}_{\\rm H} may change, and how the overall number of BHs in comparison to NSs may vary for changes in the wind mass-loss rates of stars, convective boundary mixing and the {}^{12}C(\\alpha,\\gamma)^{16}{\\rm O} nuclear reaction rate. There are further physical processes and other uncertain ingredients in stellar models that may affect the BH mass spectrum but we limit our discussion to the aforementioned aspects.

Wind mass-loss rates \\dot{M} are still considerably uncertain and could in reality be lower or higher compared to what is assumed in our models (Smith, 2014). Similarly, mass-loss rates of stars are higher at higher metallicity and lower at smaller metallicity. The indicated changes in Fig. 6a are thus meant to cover systematic uncertainties in \\dot{M} and stars at different metallicities. In this work, we have shown that BH{}_{\\rm L} and the lower end of BH{}_{\\rm H} masses depend only mildly on \\dot{M}, and that they are systematically smaller for higher \\dot{M} and thus larger metallicity (and vice versa). In contrast, the maximum BH mass depends strongly on the wind strength and metallicity, with BH masses being larger at lower \\dot{M} and smaller metallicity, and vice versa (Vink & de Koter, 2005; Belczynski et al., 2010; Spera et al., 2015).

In our models, we consider extra mixing at the boundary of convective cores during core hydrogen and core helium burning. This extra mixing is parametrized as step overshooting by an amount of f_{\\rm ov} as measured in units of the local pressure scale height. In BSSs, i.e. in helium stars, the overshooting during core helium burning sets the size of the resulting CO-core mass and thus the ratio of total helium-star to CO-core mass. Larger overshooting then implies a smaller total and hence smaller BH mass for the characteristic CO-core masses that result in low-mass BH{}_{\\rm L} and the lowest-mass BH{}_{\\rm H} (Fig. 6b). For the highest-mass BHs, larger overshooting during both core hydrogen and even more so during core helium burning imply larger stellar luminosity for a given total mass, hence higher wind mass loss and smaller BH masses.

Step overshooting during core hydrogen burning sets the helium-core masses of stars. It thus links the helium masses of BSSs to their initial mass and thus to their overall fraction as given by the stellar initial mass function. A larger overshooting value means that initially less massive stars can produce the same helium-star mass; hence, the overall fraction of BHs increases with larger overshooting during core hydrogen burning (Fig. 6b). In BSSs, overshooting during core hydrogen burning does not directly affect the resulting BH{}_{\\rm L} and BH{}_{\\rm H} masses as they are rather connected to the CO-core masses set by core helium burning.

The {}^{12}{\\rm C}(\\alpha,\\gamma)^{16}{\\rm O} nuclear reaction rate r_{{}^{12}{\\rm C}} governs the abundance of {}^{12}{\\rm C} remaining at the end of core helium burning (Langer, 1989; Woosley et al., 1993; Brown et al., 2001). A faster reaction rate converts more carbon into oxygen and vice versa. Fewer carbon atoms during core carbon burning mean that neutrinos overcome energy generation from nuclear burning already at lower CO-core masses and so the compactness peak systematically shifts to lower CO-core masses. This in turn results in less massive compactness-peak BHs (Fig. 6c). The abundance of neon after core carbon burning is less if there are fewer carbon atoms after helium burning. Hence, also the increase of compactness related to neutrinos overcoming the energy generation from core neon burning is found at lower CO-core masses such that the corresponding BH masses are smaller.

The maximum BH mass shown in Fig. 6 is connected to wind mass loss and the occurrence of pair-instability supernovae (PISNe). Assuming that it is set by PISNe that leave no BH remnants, we indicate in Fig. 6 the finding of (Farmer et al., 2020) that the lower end of the PISN BH-mass gap is at higher masses for a slower r_{{}^{12}{\\rm C}} and vice versa. The masses of BHs beyond the PISN mass gap are not shown.

Quantitatively, decreasing Z by a factor of 10 as done in this work, i.e. decreasing \\dot{M} by about a factor of 3, decreases M_{\\rm CO} of the compactness peak by 0.5\\,{\\rm M}_{\\odot} in single stars and 0.8\\,{\\rm M}_{\\odot} in BSSs. In the BSSs, this translates into an increase of the mass of compactness-peak BHs by a similar amount (\\approx\\!0.6\\,{\\rm M}_{\\odot}).

Increasing and decreasing the overshooting parameter in our single-star models by factors of 2, we find that the compactness-peak M_{\\rm CO} increases by \\approx\\!0.5\\,{\\rm M}_{\\odot} and decreases by \\approx\\!0.3\\,{\\rm M}_{\\odot}, respectively. As for the change in Z, we expect a similar change in the mass of compactness-peak BHs of BSSs.

When using the nuclear reaction rate r_{{}^{12}{\\rm C}} of (Kunz et al., 2002), which is about 20% slower than the one used in our models (Xu et al., 2013), the M_{\\rm CO} of the compactness peak gets \\approx\\!0.9\\,{\\rm M}_{\\odot} larger. Similarly, boosting r_{{}^{12}{\\rm C}} in our models by 10%, leads to \\approx\\!0.4\\,{\\rm M}_{\\odot} smaller compactness-peak M_{\\rm CO}. Again, the corresponding BH masses of BSSs are expected to change by a similar amount of mass.